(_j1.chapters = _j1.chapters || []).push(
  { n:1, summary:"John opens with what he has heard, seen with his eyes, looked upon, and handled: the Word of life, the eternal life which was with the Father and was manifested. He declares it so that his readers may share fellowship with the apostles, whose fellowship is with the Father and his Son Jesus Christ, and that their joy may be full. The message is that God is light, and in him is no darkness at all. Claiming fellowship with him while walking in darkness is lying; walking in the light brings fellowship one with another and cleansing by the blood of Jesus Christ. Denying that we have sin deceives ourselves and makes God a liar, but if we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive us our sins and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness.",
    context:"John, traditionally the apostle and the letter's author, writes to believers troubled by teachers denying the reality of Christ in the flesh, opening with testimony of the incarnate Word of life.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 John 1:5", text:"This then is the message which we have heard of him, and declare unto you, that God is light, and in him is no darkness at all." },
      { ref:"1 John 1:7", text:"But if we walk in the light, as he is in the light, we have fellowship one with another, and the blood of Jesus Christ his Son cleanseth us from all sin." },
      { ref:"1 John 1:9", text:"If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive us our sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness." }
    ],
    takeaway:"God is light: honest confession brings fellowship with him and cleansing from sin.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How does John establish his testimony about the Word of life?", choices:["By citing the temple records","By quoting the book of Isaiah","By repeating what others told him","By what he has heard, seen with his eyes, looked upon, and handled"], a:3, why:"That which was from the beginning, which we have heard, which we have seen with our eyes, which we have looked upon, and our hands have handled, of the Word of life." },
      { q:"What contradiction does John expose in 1 John 1:6?", choices:["Claiming fellowship with God while walking in darkness","Preaching grace while rejecting mercy","Professing faith while avoiding fellowship meals","Teaching truth while refusing correction"], a:0, why:"If we say that we have fellowship with him, and walk in darkness, we lie, and do not the truth." },
      { q:"What does honest confession of sin receive, according to this chapter?", choices:["A lighter sentence from the church","Forgiveness from the God who is faithful and just, and cleansing from all unrighteousness","Only a reminder to do better","Public exemption from future temptation"], a:1, why:"If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive us our sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness." }
    ] },
  { n:2, summary:"John writes so that his little children sin not, and if any man sin, Jesus Christ the righteous is the advocate with the Father, the propitiation for our sins and for the sins of the whole world. We know that we know him if we keep his commandments; the claim to know him without obedience is a lie, and whoever abides in him must walk as he walked. The commandment is old from the beginning yet new in the true light: he that loves his brother abides in the light, while hatred leaves one in darkness. Love not the world, for the lust of the flesh, the lust of the eyes, and the pride of life are not of the Father, and the world passes away. It is the last time; many antichrists have gone out, denying that Jesus is the Christ. Believers have an anointing, must abide in what they heard from the beginning, and so abide in the Son and the Father.",
    context:"John continues his pastoral letter against secessionist teachers, binding knowing God to obedient love and marking the antichrists who deny Jesus is the Christ.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 John 2:3", text:"And hereby we do know that we know him, if we keep his commandments." },
      { ref:"1 John 2:6", text:"He that saith he abideth in him ought himself also so to walk, even as he walked." },
      { ref:"1 John 2:15", text:"Love not the world, neither the things that are in the world. If any man love the world, the love of the Father is not in him." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Knowing God shows in obedience and love; the world and its lusts are already passing away.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Who is the advocate with the Father when a believer sins?", choices:["An angel of the presence","The elders of the church","Jesus Christ the righteous","The believer's own conscience"], a:2, why:"If any man sin, we have an advocate with the Father, Jesus Christ the righteous: and he is the propitiation for our sins." },
      { q:"What happens to a person who claims to be in the light yet hates his brother?", choices:["He is still in the light but weak","He deceives only himself temporarily","He must start again with baptism","He is in darkness even until now, and darkness hath blinded his eyes"], a:3, why:"He that saith he is in the light, and hateth his brother, is in darkness even until now, because that darkness hath blinded his eyes." },
      { q:"By what test does John say a person can know that he knows God?", choices:["Keeping his commandments","Reciting the creeds accurately","Attending worship each week","Retreating from the world entirely"], a:0, why:"And hereby we do know that we know him, if we keep his commandments: he that saith, I know him, and keepeth not his commandments, is a liar." }
    ] },
  { n:3, summary:"John marvels at the Father's love in calling believers the sons of God; the world does not know them because it did not know him. What they shall be does not yet appear, but when he appears they shall be like him, and everyone with this hope purifies himself, even as Christ is pure. Sin is the transgression of the law, and Christ was manifested to take away sins and destroy the works of the devil; whoever commits sin is of the devil, while whoever is born of God does not commit sin. Children of God and children of the devil are known by righteousness and love: love one another, not like Cain, who slew his brother because his own works were evil. Hatred is murder, but Christ laid down his life for us, and we ought to lay down our lives for the brethren, loving not in word or tongue but in deed and in truth.",
    context:"John unfolds the privileges of God's children: hope at Christ's appearing, freedom from sin's practice, and the family likeness of love.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 John 3:1", text:"Behold, what manner of love the Father hath bestowed upon us, that we should be called the sons of God: therefore the world knoweth us not, because it knew him not." },
      { ref:"1 John 3:16", text:"Hereby perceive we the love of God, because he laid down his life for us: and we ought to lay down our lives for the brethren." },
      { ref:"1 John 3:18", text:"My little children, let us not love in word, neither in tongue; but in deed and in truth." }
    ],
    takeaway:"God's children grow like him: purify yourself in hope, and love in deed and truth, not word alone.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What does John say believers shall be like when he shall appear?", choices:["Like angels in shining garments","Like him, for we shall see him as he is","Like the prophets who saw God","Like the world's honored dead"], a:1, why:"We know that, when he shall appear, we shall be like him; for we shall see him as he is." },
      { q:"Why does John bring up Cain in a chapter about love?", choices:["Cain shows how to recover from failure","Cain kept the first family's worship correctly","Cain slew his brother because his own works were evil and his brother's righteous, marking hatred's fruit","Cain founded the first city of refuge"], a:2, why:"Not as Cain, who was of that wicked one, and slew his brother; whosoever hateth his brother is a murderer." },
      { q:"How does John say Christ's sacrifice should shape a believer's love?", choices:["Love should be expressed only in words","Love should be reserved for family first","Love waits until others love us back","Because he laid down his life for us, we ought to lay down our lives for the brethren"], a:3, why:"Hereby perceive we the love of God, because he laid down his life for us: and we ought to lay down our lives for the brethren." }
    ] },
  { n:4, summary:"Believers are not to believe every spirit but to try the spirits, because many false prophets are gone out. Every spirit that confesses that Jesus Christ is come in the flesh is of God, and every spirit that denies it is the spirit of antichrist. Love is the other mark: love is of God, and everyone that loves is born of God and knows God, for God is love. His love was manifested when he sent his only begotten Son into the world that we might live through him, and sent his Son to be the propitiation for our sins. If God so loved us, we ought also to love one another; in loving one another God dwells in us and his love is perfected. Perfect love gives boldness in the day of judgment and casts out fear, because fear has torment. We love him because he first loved us, and whoever claims to love God while hating his brother is a liar.",
    context:"John turns from the worldly hostility of false teachers to the sure mark of God's people: love rooted in God's own love in sending his Son.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 John 4:8", text:"He that loveth not knoweth not God; for God is love." },
      { ref:"1 John 4:10", text:"Herein is love, not that we loved God, but that he loved us, and sent his Son to be the propitiation for our sins." },
      { ref:"1 John 4:18", text:"There is no fear in love; but perfect love casteth out fear: because fear hath torment. He that feareth is not made perfect in love." }
    ],
    takeaway:"God is love: because he loved us first, perfected love casts out fear and loves the brother in view.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What confession marks the Spirit of God, according to 1 John 4?", choices:["That Jesus Christ is come in the flesh","That the law endures unchanged","That Israel remains God's only people","That angels visit the faithful"], a:0, why:"Every spirit that confesseth that Jesus Christ is come in the flesh is of God; every spirit that confesseth him not is the spirit of antichrist." },
      { q:"Why does perfect love cast out fear?", choices:["Fear is merely an unpleasant feeling","Fear hath torment, and love made perfect gives boldness in the day of judgment","Love ignores consequences entirely","Fear belongs only to unbelievers"], a:1, why:"There is no fear in love; but perfect love casteth out fear: because fear hath torment. He that feareth is not made perfect in love." },
      { q:"Why can a person not love God while hating his brother?", choices:["Hatred is punished by the congregation","God refuses worship from imperfect people","He that loveth not his brother whom he hath seen cannot love God whom he hath not seen","Brothers must earn love before God grants it"], a:2, why:"If a man say, I love God, and hateth his brother, he is a liar: for he that loveth not his brother whom he hath seen, how can he love God whom he hath not seen?" }
    ] },
  { n:5, summary:"Whoever believes that Jesus is the Christ is born of God, and loving the Father means loving his children and keeping his commandments, which are not grievous. Whatever is born of God overcomes the world, and the victory that overcomes the world is our faith in Jesus the Son of God, who came by water and blood; the Father, the Word, and the Holy Ghost bear record in heaven, and the Spirit, the water, and the blood agree in one on earth. God's witness of his Son is greater than the witness of men: the record is that God has given us eternal life, and this life is in his Son; he that hath the Son hath life. John writes so that believers may know they have eternal life and may have confidence that God hears requests made according to his will. The one born of God keeps himself, and the wicked one touches him not; so let the children keep themselves from idols.",
    context:"In his closing chapter John binds faith, obedience, love, and assurance together, so that believers may know they have eternal life.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 John 5:3", text:"For this is the love of God, that we keep his commandments: and his commandments are not grievous." },
      { ref:"1 John 5:4", text:"For whatsoever is born of God overcometh the world: and this is the victory that overcometh the world, even our faith." },
      { ref:"1 John 5:13", text:"These things have I written unto you that believe on the name of the Son of God; that ye may know that ye have eternal life, and that ye may believe on the name of the Son of God." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Faith in God's Son overcomes the world and carries the assurance of eternal life.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What does John call the victory that overcometh the world?", choices:["Steadfast obedience","Fervent prayer","Brotherly kindness","Even our faith"], a:3, why:"Whatsoever is born of God overcometh the world: and this is the victory that overcometh the world, even our faith." },
      { q:"Why does John say he wrote this closing testimony?", choices:["That believers may know that they have eternal life","That churches might preserve his handwriting","That the scoffers might be answered","That missionaries might travel farther"], a:0, why:"These things have I written unto you that believe on the name of the Son of God; that ye may know that ye have eternal life." },
      { q:"What confidence in prayer does 1 John 5 describe?", choices:["Loud and repeated requests are always answered","If we ask any thing according to his will, he heareth us","Fasting guarantees every petition","Only apostles' prayers are heard directly"], a:1, why:"This is the confidence that we have in him, that, if we ask any thing according to his will, he heareth us." }
    ] }
);
